itive domain (language, visuospatial, executive) that interfere with daily function, with reversible causes excluded and no features suggesting another dementia.", "items": ["Insidious onset", "Progressive worsening", "Amnestic plus another cognitive domain affected", "Functional decline", "Reversible causes excluded", "No features of another dementia (e.g., early visual hallucinations, parkinsonism before memory loss, stepwise decline)"]}], "scores": ["mmse", "moca"]}, "differential": [{"name": "Delirium", "slug": "delirium", "why_considered": "Acute confusion in an older adult is often mistaken for dementia, and delirium can be superimposed on Alzheimer's disease", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Abrupt onset over hours to days with fluctuating alertness", "discriminators": {"history": ["Onset over hours to days", "Fluctuating course through the day", "Recent infection, surgery, new medication, or hospitalization", "Inattention and easy distractibility"], "exam": ["Disoriented with waxing and waning attention", "Tremor or myoclonus if withdrawal or metabolic cause", "Often restless or drowsy rather than calmly forgetful"], "labs": ["Abnormal CBC, BMP, urinalysis, or TSH pointing to a medical cause"], "imaging": ["Head CT or MRI to exclude acute structural cause"]}}, {"name": "Depression with cognitive complaints (pseudodementia)", "slug": "major-depressive-disorder", "why_considered": "Late-life depression causes poor concentration, apathy, and memory complaints that resemble early Alzheimer's disease", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Prominent depressed mood, anhedonia, or recent loss with cognitive complaints", "discriminators": {"history": ["Sad mood, hopelessness, or loss of interest most of the day", "Symptoms often date to a stressful event or bereavement", "Patient emphasizes memory problems and may answer 'I don't know'", "Sleep and appetite changes"], "exam": ["Depressed affect, psychomotor slowing", "Cognitive effort is poor but improves with encouragement and cueing", "Mini-Mental State Examination scores may vary between visits"], "labs": ["TSH and B12 to exclude reversible contributors"], "imaging": ["MRI brain usually without the medial temporal atrophy pattern of Alzheimer's disease"]}}, {"name": "Normal pressure hydrocephalus", "slug": "normal-pressure-hydrocephalus", "why_considered": "Gait disturbance, urinary incontinence, and cognitive slowing can be mistaken for dementia", "must_not_miss": true, "trigger": "Gait apraxia and urinary incontinence early in the cognitive decline", "discriminators": {"history": ["Gait difficulty often precedes memory loss", "Urinary urgency and incontinence early", "Magnetic gait with feet seeming stuck to the floor"], "exam": ["Wide-based, shuffling gait with difficulty turning", "Cognitive deficits less prominent than gait"], "labs": ["No specific blood test; diagnosis is clinical and radiologic"], "imaging": ["Ventriculomegaly out of proportion to cortical atrophy on CT or MRI"]}}, {"name": "Vascular dementia", "slug": "vascular-dementia", "why_considered": "Second most common cause of dementia and frequently coexists with Alzheimer's disease", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Stepwise decline with sudden drops after strokes", "Focal neurologic symptoms or prior transient ischemic attack", "Vascular risk factors such as hypertension, diabetes, smoking"], "exam": ["Focal deficits, asymmetric reflexes, or gait disturbance", "Executive dysfunction may be more prominent than memory loss"], "labs": ["A1c, lipids, and glucose to assess vascular risk"], "imaging": ["Multiple lacunar infarcts, white matter hyperintensities, or cortical infarcts on MRI"]}}, {"name": "Dementia with Lewy bodies", "slug": "dementia-with-lewy-bodies", "why_considered": "Progressive cognitive decline in an older adult with behavioral symptoms", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Visual hallucinations early in the illness", "Fluctuating alertness and attention", "REM sleep behavior disorder with acting out dreams", "Parkinsonism before or around the time memory loss begins"], "exam": ["Resting tremor, rigidity, or bradykinesia", "Marked sensitivity to antipsychotic medications"], "labs": ["No specific blood test"], "imaging": ["Reduced dopamine transporter uptake on DaTscan; relative preservation of medial temporal lobes on MRI"]}}, {"name": "Frontotemporal dementia", "slug": "frontotemporal-dementia", "why_considered": "Personality change and language difficulty in a patient with cognitive decline", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Prominent personality change, disinhibition, or apathy early", "Language difficulty with word-finding or comprehension early", "Memory relatively preserved early", "Younger age at onset, often 45 to 65"], "exam": ["Executive dysfunction with relatively preserved memory", "Socially inappropriate behavior"], "labs": ["No specific blood test"], "imaging": ["Frontal and anterior temporal atrophy on MRI or hypometabolism on FDG-PET"]}}, {"name": "Medication-induced cognitive impairment", "slug": "medication-induced-cognitive-impairment", "why_considered": "Anticholinergics, benzodiazepines, and diphenhydramine commonly cause memory and attention problems in older adults", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Symptoms began or worsened after starting a new medication", "Use of anticholinergics, sedatives, or opioids", "Improvement after stopping the medication"], "exam": ["Attention and memory deficits that fluctuate with dosing", "No focal neurologic findings"], "labs": ["Drug levels if applicable; otherwise normal workup"], "imaging": ["MRI brain without atrophy pattern typical of Alzheimer's disease"]}}, {"name": "Hypothyroidism", "slug": "hypothyroidism", "why_considered": "Reversible cause of cognitive slowing, memory complaints, and apathy", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Fatigue, cold intolerance, constipation, weight gain", "Slowly progressive cognitive slowing"], "exam": ["Dry skin, bradycardia, delayed reflexes, goiter"], "labs": ["Elevated TSH with low free T4"], "imaging": ["Not required for diagnosis"]}}, {"name": "Vitamin B12 deficiency", "slug": "vitamin-b12-deficiency", "why_considered": "Reversible cause of memory loss, neuropathy, and cognitive decline", "must_not_miss": false, "discriminators": {"history": ["Paresthesias, imbalance, or glossitis", "Vegan diet, bariatric surgery, or metformin use", "Macrocytic anemia"], "exam": ["Decreased vibration and proprioception", "Impaired tandem gait", "Hyperreflexia or extensor plantar response"], "labs": ["Low serum B12, elevated methylmalonic acid and homocysteine", "Macrocytosis on CBC"], "imaging": ["Dorsal column changes on MRI in severe cases"]}}], "course": {"severity": {"mild": "Early stage: short-term memory lapses, repeating questions, misplacing items, and trouble with finances or medications, but the patient remains independent in basic self-care and may still drive with supervision.", "moderate": "Middle stage: disorientation to time and place, word-finding difficulty, personality change, wandering, and needing help with dressing, bathing, and managing money; behavioral symptoms such as agitation or delusions may appear.", "severe": "Late stage: loss of ability to communicate, recognize family, or perform any activities of daily living; incontinence, gait apraxia, swallowing difficulty, and total dependence on caregivers.", "critical": "End stage with immobility, contractures, mutism, and inability to swallow, leading to aspiration pneumonia, pressure ulcers, and sepsis; death usually results from these complications rather than the disease itself."}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Abrupt onset or stepwise decline over hours to days", "meaning": "Suggests delirium, stroke, or another acute process rather than Alzheimer's disease"}, {"finding": "Focal weakness, numbness, or a new single-sided deficit", "meaning": "Suggests stroke or mass lesion; needs urgent imaging"}, {"finding": "New seizures", "meaning": "Requires evaluation for structural lesion, infection, or metabolic cause"}, {"finding": "Prominent visual hallucinations early in the illness", "meaning": "Suggests dementia with Lewy bodies rather than Alzheimer's disease"}, {"finding": "Parkinsonism before memory loss", "meaning": "Suggests Parkinson disease dementia or Lewy body dementia"}, {"finding": "Fluctuating alertness from hour to hour", "meaning": "Suggests delirium or Lewy body dementia"}, {"finding": "Recent head trauma", "meaning": "Suggests subdural hematoma; needs urgent imaging"}, {"finding": "Fever or systemic illness", "meaning": "Suggests infection or delirium superimposed on dementia"}, {"finding": "New incontinence early in the illness", "meaning": "Suggests normal pressure hydrocephalus or another structural cause"}, {"finding": "Symptoms that began while starting a new medication", "meaning": "Suggests medication-induced cognitive impairment"}], "complications": [{"name": "Wandering and getting lost", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Patient found away from home", "Caregiver reports repeated episodes", "Unfamiliar settings trigger it"]}, {"name": "Falls and fractures", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Gait apraxia and unsteadiness", "Prior fall", "Use of sedating medications"]}, {"name": "Malnutrition and dehydration", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Weight loss", "Poor appetite", "Refusal to eat or drink"]}, {"name": "Aspiration pneumonia", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Coughing or choking with meals", "Dysphagia", "Fever and hypoxia"]}, {"name": "Pressure ulcers", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Immobility", "Incontinence", "Poor nutrition"]}, {"name": "Urinary tract infection and sepsis", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Incontinence", "Fever", "Acute confusion"]}, {"name": "Delirium superimposed on dementia", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Acute worsening of confusion", "New medication, infection, or hospitalization"]}, {"name": "Caregiver burnout", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Caregiver reports exhaustion, depression, or inability to continue care"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Apathy, gait slowing, and withdrawal may dominate while the patient denies memory problems; family reports the decline. Comorbidities and polypharmacy increase the risk of delirium."}, {"population": "children", "differences": "Not applicable; Alzheimer's disease does not occur in children."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "When did the memory problems start, and how have they changed over time?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Insidious onset over months to years with steady worsening; abrupt onset over hours to days suggests delirium"}, {"question": "What kinds of things does he or she forget — recent conversations and appointments, or mostly names and where the keys are?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Forgetting recent events and conversations, repeating the same question within minutes — an amnestic pattern beyond normal aging"}, {"question": "Can he or she still manage money, bills, medications, and appointments without help?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Loss of independence in instrumental activities — this separates dementia from mild cognitive impairment"}, {"question": "Has he or she gotten lost in familiar places or had trouble driving?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Getting lost on routes driven for years; a marker of visuospatial and functional decline"}, {"question": "Is there trouble finding words, following a conversation, or reading and writing?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Language involvement beyond memory, supporting deficits in more than one cognitive domain"}, {"question": "Has his or her personality or mood changed — apathy, irritability, or withdrawal from hobbies?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Apathy, irritability, or loss of interest; common behavioral features of Alzheimer's disease"}, {"question": "Has he or she had any hallucinations, or does he or she act out dreams at night?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Prominent early visual hallucinations or dream enactment suggest dementia with Lewy bodies instead"}, {"question": "Has the decline been stepwise, with sudden drops after a stroke or hospitalization?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Stepwise decline suggests vascular contribution rather than the smooth progression of Alzheimer's disease"}, {"question": "Has there been a recent fall, head injury, or new seizure?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Subdural hematoma, structural lesion, or another acute cause requiring urgent imaging"}, {"question": "What medications is he or she taking, including over-the-counter sleep or allergy medicines?", "category": "medications", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Anticholinergics, benzodiazepines, or diphenhydramine that can cause or worsen cognitive impairment"}, {"question": "Has anyone in the family had dementia or Alzheimer's disease, especially before age 65?", "category": "family", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "A first-degree relative with dementia; rare early-onset familial cases with multiple affected relatives"}, {"question": "Who does he or she live with, and who helps with daily tasks?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Lives alone or with a spouse who has become the caregiver; caregiver stress and safety at home"}, {"question": "Does he or she smoke, drink alcohol, or use any recreational drugs?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Alcohol or substance use as an alternative or contributing cause of cognitive decline"}, {"question": "Has he or she had a fever, recent infection, or new illness?", "category": "ros", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Infection or systemic illness suggesting delirium superimposed on dementia"}], "accepted_names": ["Alzheimer's disease", "Alzheimer disease", "AD", "Dementia of the Alzheimer type", "Probable Alzheimer's disease dementia"], "reasoning_points": ["Insidious onset over months to years with steadily progressive decline", "Short-term memory impairment plus at least one other cognitive domain (language, visuospatial, or executive)", "Functional decline — the patient needs help with finances, medications, driving, or appointments", "Reversible causes excluded: TSH, B12, folate, electrolytes, calcium, glucose/A1c, and a medication review", "Neuroimaging excludes structural causes such as tumor, stroke, subdural hematoma, or hydrocephalus", "No features pointing to another dementia: no early visual hallucinations, no parkinsonism before memory loss, no stepwise decline, no early prominent gait disorder or incontinence", "Collateral history from a family member is essential because the patient may underreport deficits"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Older adult over 65 with insidious, progressive short-term memory loss", "Repeats the same question during the visit", "Family reports getting lost in familiar places", "Normal focal neurologic exam with no tremor or weakness", "Reversible causes excluded on labs"], "medium": ["Patient denies any problem and the family provides the whole history", "Prominent apathy or depression that raises the question of pseudodementia", "Coexisting vascular risk factors and white matter changes on MRI", "Mild cognitive impairment at the borderline of dementia", "Medication list includes an anticholinergic that must be sorted out"], "hard": ["Early-onset disease before age 65 with a strong family history", "Atypical presentation with language or visuospatial deficits dominating over memory", "Delirium superimposed on dementia after a hospitalization", "Oldest-old patient with gait slowing and withdrawal but no self-reported memory complaint", "Diagnostic uncertainty requiring FDG-PET or amyloid PET to distinguish from frontotemporal or Lewy body dementia"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
