may be low or absent and the white count can be near normal even with perforation. Perforation rates are higher."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Appendicitis is the most common non-obstetric surgical emergency in pregnancy. The growing uterus pushes the appendix upward, so pain may be in the right flank or upper right abdomen, and nausea and vomiting may be attributed to pregnancy. Ultrasound first, then MRI if nondiagnostic."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "A thicker abdominal wall makes the exam less reliable and imaging harder to interpret; pain may feel less localized and the exam less impressive than the underlying disease."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "May have blunted fever and leukocytosis and can progress rapidly to perforation; maintain a lower threshold for imaging."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "Where did the pain start, and has it moved since then?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Pain that began around the belly button and then settled in the right lower abdomen"}, {"question": "When did the pain start, and is it getting better or worse?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Gradual onset over hours, steadily worsening, usually less than 48 hours"}, {"question": "Is the pain constant or does it come and go?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Constant once it localizes, with no pain-free stretches"}, {"question": "Does anything make it worse, like walking, coughing, or going over bumps in the car?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Pain worse with movement or jarring, suggesting peritoneal irritation"}, {"question": "Does anything make it feel better?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Lying still with the knees bent"}, {"question": "Did the vomiting start before or after the pain began?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", "