nability to speak, cyanosis, oxygen saturation below 90%, and a normal or rising PaCO2 in a distressed patient indicating impending respiratory failure"}, "red_flags": [{"finding": "Silent chest with minimal or absent breath sounds", "meaning": "Airflow is critically low; impending respiratory arrest"}, {"finding": "Normal or rising PaCO2 in a distressed patient", "meaning": "The patient is tiring and heading toward respiratory failure; this is not reassuring"}, {"finding": "Drowsiness, confusion, or inability to speak", "meaning": "Hypoxemia and hypercapnia; needs immediate airway support"}, {"finding": "Cyanosis or oxygen saturation below 90% on room air", "meaning": "Severe hypoxemia"}, {"finding": "Use of accessory muscles, tripod position, or intercostal retractions", "meaning": "Severe increased work of breathing"}, {"finding": "Heart rate above 120 or respiratory rate above 30", "meaning": "Severe flare requiring urgent treatment"}, {"finding": "Poor response to repeated albuterol doses", "meaning": "Refractory bronchospasm; consider systemic corticosteroids and possible admission"}, {"finding": "Peak expiratory flow below 50% of personal best", "meaning": "Severe obstruction"}], "complications": [{"name": "Acute severe asthma flare requiring emergency care", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Progressive dyspnea over hours to days", "Poor response to rescue inhaler", "Accessory muscle use"]}, {"name": "Respiratory failure with hypercapnia", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Drowsiness", "Rising PaCO2", "Silent chest"]}, {"name": "Pneumothorax", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "life_threatening", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Sudden unilateral pleuritic chest pain", "Asymmetric breath sounds", "Tracheal deviation in tension pneumothorax"]}, {"name": "Pneumomediastinum", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "rare", "clues": ["Sudden chest or neck pain", "Subcutaneous crepitus", "Hamman sign on auscultation"]}, {"name": "Atelectasis from mucus plugging", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Persistent hypoxemia despite bronchodilators", "Focal decreased breath sounds", "New opacity on chest x-ray"]}, {"name": "Airway remodeling with fixed airflow limitation", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Long-standing poorly controlled asthma", "Spirometry that no longer fully reverses with bronchodilators"]}, {"name": "Corticosteroid adverse effects", "timing": "late", "seriousness": "serious", "frequency": "sometimes", "clues": ["Frequent oral corticosteroid courses", "Weight gain, hyperglycemia, osteoporosis, cataracts, or adrenal suppression"]}, {"name": "Sleep disruption and impaired quality of life", "timing": "any", "seriousness": "minor", "frequency": "common", "clues": ["Frequent nighttime awakenings", "Missed school or work", "Reduced exercise tolerance"]}], "special_populations": [{"population": "children", "differences": "Young children may not wheeze audibly and instead present with recurrent cough, night waking, poor feeding, or exercise limitation. Spirometry is feasible from about age 5-6; younger children are managed on symptoms and response to a trial of controller therapy. Caregiver education on inhaler technique with a spacer and mask is essential."}, {"population": "older_adults", "differences": "Symptoms may be attributed to aging or COPD, and heart failure is a common mimic. Spirometry is needed to separate asthma from COPD and cardiac disease, and comorbidities and polypharmacy increase the risk of drug interactions."}, {"population": "pregnancy", "differences": "Symptoms can improve, worsen, or stay the same. Uncontrolled asthma is more dangerous to the fetus than controller therapy, so inhaled corticosteroids should be continued. Dyspnea of pregnancy and reflux cough can mimic asthma, so objective testing is needed before changing therapy. Check a pregnancy test before imaging or systemic corticosteroids in anyone who could be pregnant."}, {"population": "obesity", "differences": "Dyspnea and exercise limitation are common from deconditioning and weight, so symptoms may be blamed on weight rather than asthma. Spirometry is essential, and weight loss improves control."}, {"population": "diabetes", "differences": "Systemic corticosteroids during flares can cause marked hyperglycemia; monitor glucose closely and adjust diabetes therapy when oral steroids are used."}, {"population": "renal_impairment", "differences": "Not a major modifier of asthma itself, but monitor electrolytes and renal function when systemic corticosteroids or frequent beta-agonists are used."}, {"population": "immunocompromised", "differences": "Infections are a more likely cause of new wheeze and dyspnea; lower the threshold for chest imaging and infectious workup before attributing symptoms to asthma."}]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "What do your symptoms feel like — wheezing, chest tightness, cough, or shortness of breath?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Wheeze, chest tightness, dry cough, and dyspnea, often in combination"}, {"question": "Do the symptoms come and go, or are they there all the time?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Episodic and variable over time, with symptom-free intervals between flares"}, {"question": "What seems to set them off?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Exercise, cold air, dust, pollen, pets, mold, smoke, strong odors, or viral colds"}, {"question": "Are they worse at night or early in the morning?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Nocturnal or early-morning worsening, a hallmark of asthma"}, {"question": "How often do symptoms occur, and how many nights a month do they wake you?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Frequency of daytime symptoms and nighttime awakenings, which grade control and severity"}, {"question": "What makes them better?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Improvement with a rescue inhaler, rest, or avoiding the trigger"}, {"question": "Do you have a history of allergies, eczema, or hay fever?", "category": "pmh", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Atopic history supporting an allergic phenotype"}, {"question": "Does anyone in your family have asthma, allergies, or eczema?", "category": "family", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Family history of asthma or atopy, especially in a parent"}, {"question": "What inhalers or other medicines do you use, and how often do you actually use them?", "category": "medications", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Rescue versus controller inhaler use, frequency, and adherence"}, {"question": "Do you take aspirin, ibuprofen, or naproxen, and do they make your breathing worse?", "category": "medications", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "NSAID sensitivity suggesting aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease"}, {"question": "Does anyone smoke at home, or do you smoke or vape?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Tobacco or secondhand smoke exposure, a common trigger and worsening factor"}, {"question": "Are there pets, mold, cockroaches, or mice where you live?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Indoor allergen exposures that can be reduced"}, {"question": "What kind of work do you do, and are symptoms better on days away from work?", "category": "social", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Occupational exposure with improvement away from work, suggesting occupational asthma"}, {"question": "Have you had fever, chills, or coughed up blood or colored sputum?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Absent in typical asthma; present suggests pneumonia or another cause"}, {"question": "Have you had sudden chest pain, leg swelling, or a choking episode?", "category": "red_flag", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Absent; present raises concern for pneumothorax, pulmonary embolism, or foreign body aspiration"}, {"question": "Have you ever been intubated, admitted, or treated in the emergency department for your breathing?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Prior severe flares, which predict future risk"}, {"question": "Do you have heartburn, postnasal drip, or nasal congestion?", "category": "associated", "importance": "optional", "looking_for": "Reflux or allergic rhinitis that can worsen control"}], "accepted_names": ["Asthma", "Bronchial asthma", "Reactive airway disease", "Exercise-induced asthma", "Exercise-induced bronchoconstriction", "Allergic asthma", "Atopic asthma"], "reasoning_points": ["Recurrent, episodic wheeze, cough, chest tightness, or dyspnea that varies over time", "Symptoms worse at night or early morning and provoked by typical triggers such as exercise, allergens, cold air, or viral infections", "Personal or family history of atopy (allergic rhinitis, eczema, food allergy)", "Between episodes the patient may feel well and the lung exam may be normal", "Objective evidence of variable expiratory airflow limitation, such as a reduced FEV1/FVC ratio that improves after a short-acting bronchodilator on spirometry", "Alternative causes of dyspnea and wheeze considered and excluded, including pneumonia, heart failure, COPD, pulmonary embolism, and foreign body aspiration"], "difficulty": {"easy": ["Child or young adult with a long history of episodic wheeze", "Clear trigger pattern and nocturnal worsening", "Known atopy or family history of asthma", "Diffuse expiratory wheeze on exam and improvement with albuterol"], "medium": ["Symptoms mainly with exercise, with a normal exam between episodes", "Cough-variant presentation with chronic nocturnal cough and little wheeze", "Adult with new symptoms that could be COPD or heart failure", "Symptoms during a viral illness that persist after the infection clears"], "hard": ["Older adult with dyspnea and wheeze later attributed to heart failure", "Toddler with recurrent cough and no audible wheeze", "Patient with symptoms that persist despite escalating therapy because of poor inhaler technique or nonadherence", "Pregnant patient with dyspnea that could be dyspnea of pregnancy or reflux", "Occupational presentation with symptoms only at work"]}}}$rounds_kb$::jsonb)
