r than carcinoma, so tissue diagnosis is essential."}]}, "cancer": {"screening": "The US Preventive Services Task Force recommends biennial screening mammography for women aged 40 to 74 years. Women at higher risk because of a known BRCA mutation, prior chest radiation, or a strong family history may begin earlier and use MRI in addition to mammography.", "pathology": "Most breast cancers are adenocarcinomas arising from the ductal or lobular epithelium. Ductal carcinoma in situ is non-invasive; invasive ductal carcinoma is the most common invasive type. Lobular carcinoma is more often bilateral. Inflammatory breast cancer and Paget disease of the nipple are less common variants. Receptor status (estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, HER2) and grade guide treatment.", "staging_concepts": "Staging uses the TNM system: tumor size and chest wall or skin involvement (T), regional lymph node involvement (N), and distant metastasis (M). Stages I through III are non-metastatic; stage IV is metastatic disease. Early-stage disease is often detected by screening before a palpable mass is present.", "metastatic_sites": ["bone", "liver", "lung", "brain", "distant lymph nodes"]}, "teaching": {"key_questions": [{"question": "When did you first notice the lump, and has it changed since then?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Gradual onset over weeks to months; persistent or slowly enlarging rather than coming and going"}, {"question": "Does the lump change with your menstrual cycle, or is it there all the time?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "essential", "looking_for": "Constant and non-cyclical; fluctuation with periods points to benign fibrocystic change"}, {"question": "Is the lump painful?", "category": "hpi", "importance": "important", "looking_for": "Usually painless; mild non-cyclical pain is possible"}, {"question": "Have you noticed any change in the size or shape of that breast?", "category": "associated", "importance": "important", 